.\" getdelim.3 - delimited string input
.TH GETDELIM 3 "2026-10-03" "Substrate" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
getline, getdelim \- delimited string input
.SH LIBRARY
.B libc
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <stdio.h>
.PP
.BI "ssize_t getline(char **" lineptr ", size_t *" n ", FILE *" stream ");"
.PP
.BI "ssize_t getdelim(char **" lineptr ", size_t *" n ", int " delimiter ", FILE *" stream ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR getdelim ()
reads from
.I stream
up to and including the first occurrence of
.IR delimiter ,
or to end of file, and stores the bytes read in the buffer
.IR *lineptr ,
followed by a terminating null byte.
.PP
If
.I *lineptr
is NULL or
.I *n
is zero on entry, a buffer is allocated with
.BR malloc (3);
otherwise
.I *lineptr
must point to a buffer of
.I *n
bytes obtained from
.BR malloc (3).
The buffer is enlarged with
.BR realloc (3)
as needed, and
.I *lineptr
and
.I *n
are updated to describe it.
The caller frees it with
.BR free (3),
also after a failed call.
.PP
.BR getline ()
is
.BR getdelim ()
with a
.I delimiter
of newline.
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success, both return the number of bytes read, including the delimiter
but not the terminating null byte.
They return \-1 on error, and at end of file when no bytes were read.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EINVAL
.IR lineptr ,
.I n
or
.I stream
is NULL.
.TP
.B ENOMEM
The buffer could not be allocated or enlarged.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
char *line = NULL;
size_t cap = 0;
ssize_t len;

while ((len = getline(&line, &cap, stdin)) != \-1)
    fwrite(line, 1, (size_t)len, stdout);
free(line);
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fgets (3),
.BR fgetc (3),
.BR malloc (3)
